### 5.1 The Exponential Nature of the Blow-Up

The blow-up in the Navier-Stokes equations is not linear. It is exponential. The velocity grows as:

\[
|\mathbf{u}(t)| \sim \frac{1}{(T - t)^\gamma}
\]

where \( T \) is the blow-up time and \( \gamma \) is a critical exponent. The exponential growth is the signature of \( e \)—the constant that governs exponential processes.

This is not a coincidence. The exponential growth is the **mathematical manifestation** of the gap's accumulation. The gap grows exponentially as the model is pushed beyond its domain of validity.

### 5.2 The Critical Exponent and the Gap Constant

The critical exponent \( \gamma \) is related to the gap constant \( \mathcal{G}_c \):

\[
